.TH RMDIR 1 2026-10-20 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
rmdir \- remove empty directories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rmdir
.RB [ \-pv ]
.RB [ \-\-ignore\-fail\-on\-non\-empty ]
.IR directory ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rmdir
removes each
.I directory
operand, in order, if it is empty.
It is the
.BR busybox (1)
applet, which has fewer options than GNU
.BR rmdir ;
see
.BR NOTES .
It never removes a file, or a directory that has contents.
.B "rm \-r"
removes a tree; see
.BR rm (1).
.PP
A
.I directory
that cannot be removed is reported on standard error, and the remaining
operands are still processed.
The exit status is then 1.
An invalid option is reported on standard error.
With an invalid option or no operand,
.B rmdir
prints a usage message on standard error and exits with status 1.
.SS Parent directories
With
.BR \-p ,
after removing a directory
.B rmdir
goes on to its parent, then the parent's parent, and so on.
The parent is the path without its last component.
The walk for an operand ends after a directory that has no parent
component, such as
.I out
in
.IR out/obj ,
and at the first directory that cannot be removed.
That failure is an error like any other, so a parent that is not empty
makes the exit status 1 unless
.B \-\-ignore\-fail\-on\-non\-empty
is given.
.PP
A path with a root does not end by itself: after its topmost directory,
.B rmdir
tries the root, as in
.I c:/
or
.IR / ,
which cannot be removed.
A path that begins with
.IR .. ,
such as
.IR ../out ,
goes on to
.IR .. ,
the directory that holds the current directory, which cannot be removed.
.SS Output
Nothing is written to standard output unless
.B \-v
or
.B \-\-help
is given.
An error is written to standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
rmdir: \(aq\fIpath\fR\(aq: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
where
.I path
is the operand as typed, or with
.B \-p
the parent as cut from it, and
.I reason
is the system's message.
These are the common reasons:
.TP
.B No such file or directory
The path does not exist, the operand is empty, or the full path is
260 or more characters.
.TP
.B Directory not empty
The directory has contents.
.TP
.B Not a directory
The operand is a symbolic link or junction named without a trailing
slash; see
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.B Invalid argument
The operand is a file, or contains a character that Windows does not
allow in a name, such as
.B ?\&
or
.BR * .
.TP
.B Permission denied
The directory is in use, such as the current directory of a process, or
access to it is denied.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-pv ,
and may follow operands, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
A long option may be abbreviated to any unambiguous prefix.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-parents
Remove the parents of each directory as well; see
.BR "Parent directories" .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Write
.BI "rmdir: removing directory, \(aq" path \(aq
on standard output before each attempt to remove a directory, including
each parent tried by
.BR \-p ,
and a directory that then cannot be removed, whose error follows on
standard error.
The usage message omits this option.
.TP
.B \-\-ignore\-fail\-on\-non\-empty
Skip a directory that is not empty, without a message and without
changing the exit status.
With
.BR \-p ,
the walk up from it ends there.
Every other error is still reported.
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument only, write a usage message on standard output
and exit with status 0.
The message omits
.BR \-v ,
.BR \-\-parents ,
and
.BR \-\-verbose ,
and lists
.B \-\-ignore\-fail\-on\-non\-empty
without a description.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments are expanded when busybox starts, for a caller
such as
.B cmd.exe
that does not expand them.
When it is unset, busybox sets it to
.B 0
for the programs it starts, so commands run from the kit's shell get the
shell's expansion only.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, the first operand ends the options.
.PP
Messages written to a console also depend on
.BR BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION ,
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE ,
.BR LANG ,
.BR LC_ALL ,
and
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
and Windows error dialogs on
.BR BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS ,
as for every busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including
.B \-\-help
as the first argument, and directories skipped by
.BR \-\-ignore\-fail\-on\-non\-empty .
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option, no operand, or any directory that could not be
removed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Remove an empty directory:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ rmdir build
.EE
.RE
.PP
A directory with contents is left alone:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ rmdir src
rmdir: \(aqsrc\(aq: Directory not empty
.EE
.RE
.PP
Remove a directory and its empty parents, listing each:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ rmdir \-pv out/obj/debug
rmdir: removing directory, \(aqout/obj/debug\(aq
rmdir: removing directory, \(aqout/obj\(aq
rmdir: removing directory, \(aqout\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
Remove a directory and as many of its parents as are empty, without an
error for the first one that is not:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ rmdir \-p \-\-ignore\-fail\-on\-non\-empty out/obj/debug
.EE
.RE
.PP
Remove every empty directory under
.IR build ,
and
.I build
itself if nothing is left, discarding the errors for the others:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ find build \-depth \-type d \-exec rmdir {} + 2>/dev/null
.EE
.RE
.PP
Remove a directory and everything in it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ rm \-r build
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Unlike GNU
.BR rmdir ,
this
.B rmdir
has no
.B \-\-version
option, and
.B \-\-help
works only as the first argument.
.PP
A symbolic link or junction is refused with
.BR "Not a directory" ,
as on Linux, even when it points to a directory, and
.BR rm (1)
removes it; the target is untouched.
With a trailing slash, as in
.IR link/ ,
a junction is not detected: it is removed, even when its target has
contents, and the target is untouched.
A file is refused with
.BR "Invalid argument" .
A directory that a process uses as its current directory cannot be
removed, so
.B rmdir .\&
fails with
.BR "Permission denied" .
.PP
A read-only directory is removed.
.B rmdir
clears the read-only attribute of each path before it tries to remove
it, so a file or directory that then cannot be removed, such as a
directory that is not empty, is left without the attribute.
.PP
Paths may have drive letters and backslashes.
Both kinds of slash separate components, and messages show a path as
typed.
A path may be absolute, as in
.IR c:/dir ,
relative, or relative to the current directory of a drive, as in
.IR c:dir .
A bare drive, such as
.IR c: ,
names that directory, as does
.IR c:. ,
so on the current drive
.B rmdir c:
fails with
.BR "Permission denied" .
With
.BR \-p ,
the parent of
.I c:dir
is
.IR c:. ,
so
.B rmdir
goes on to try it instead of stopping.
.PP
Names may contain any Unicode character on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
A full path of 260 or more characters, counting the current directory
for a relative path, fails with
.BR "No such file or directory" .
.PP
From
.BR cmd.exe ,
.B rmdir
expands wildcards in its arguments itself; see
.BR BB_GLOBBING .
A pattern that matches nothing is used as it is, and fails with
.BR "Invalid argument" .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR find (1),
.BR mkdir (1),
.BR rm (1)
